⚡ European Electricity Market Forecasting Engine
AI-Based Intraday & Day-Ahead Electricity Price Forecasting Platform

An end-to-end machine learning platform for forecasting European electricity market prices across five major European markets.

The project demonstrates the complete workflow of an energy forecasting pipeline, from raw data processing and feature engineering to machine learning model training, forecasting, backtesting and interactive visualization.

⚠️ Repository Notice

This repository does not include the data/ and models/ folders.

The original project contains several datasets, trained machine learning models and forecast outputs whose combined size exceeds GitHub's recommended repository limits.

These folders have therefore been excluded from version control.

The repository contains all source code required to reproduce the complete project.

Repository Structure
├── app.py
├── pages/
├── src/
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md

The following folders are intentionally omitted:

data/
models/
How the Project Works

The project is fully reproducible because every processing step is implemented inside the src directory.

The workflow is:

Raw Data
    │
    ▼
src/preprocessing
    │
    ▼
Clean Datasets
    │
    ▼
src/features
    │
    ▼
Feature Engineering
    │
    ▼
Feature Datasets
    │
    ▼
src/models
    │
    ▼
Machine Learning Training
    │
    ▼
Saved Models
    │
    ▼
src/forecasting
    │
    ▼
Forecast Generation
    │
    ▼
src/backtesting
    │
    ▼
Performance Evaluation
    │
    ▼
Streamlit Dashboard
Reproducing the Project

The project was designed so that all missing files can be regenerated by running the source modules in sequence.

Step 1 — Data Processing

Run the preprocessing scripts to clean the raw electricity market datasets.

Output:

data/processed/
Step 2 — Feature Engineering

Run the feature engineering modules.

They create:

lag variables
rolling statistics
calendar features
weather features

Output:

data/features/
Step 3 — Train Models

Run the training scripts.

Models are saved automatically inside:

models/

Supported models:

XGBoost
Random Forest
Step 4 — Generate Forecasts

Run the forecasting modules.

They automatically create:

data/forecasts/

including:

Intraday forecasts
Day-Ahead forecasts
Step 5 — Backtesting

Run the backtesting modules.

Outputs:

data/backtesting/

including evaluation metrics such as:

MAE
RMSE
MAPE
Forecast Errors
Step 6 — Launch Dashboard

Once the previous steps have been completed:

streamlit run app.py

The Streamlit application automatically loads the generated datasets and trained models.

Dashboard Modules

